WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:04.550
The strongest physical correlation can be tracing a bed continuously between

00:00:04.550 --> 00:00:08.850
sites. Distinctive rock types, matching sequences and common relationships to an

00:00:08.850 --> 00:00:12.810
unconformity offer other evidence. A local landslide may occur in only

00:00:12.810 --> 00:00:17.370
one column. Similar-looking rocks alone are less persuasive than several independent

00:00:17.370 --> 00:00:18.730
relationships that fit together.

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:25.770
Matching index-fossil assemblages, isotopic ages and magnetic-reversal patterns can strengthen correlation.

00:00:26.090 --> 00:00:30.490
The source’s basalt ages are similar rather than numerically identical, and

00:00:30.490 --> 00:00:34.050
a magnetic pattern is suggestive rather than conclusive by itself.

00:00:34.310 --> 00:00:38.550
Use the method’s uncertainty and geological context instead of demanding the

00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:39.970
same number from every sample.

00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:45.270
Coastal environments change sideways from land through shore to deeper water.

00:00:45.430 --> 00:00:48.910
Sediment deposited at the same time can therefore become different rock

00:00:48.910 --> 00:00:54.430
types. Restricted deposition, variable thickness and later erosion also change columns.

00:00:55.130 --> 00:00:59.590
Animate sediment accumulating across environments, then account for each lateral difference

00:00:59.590 --> 00:01:01.430
before drawing a correlation line.

00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:06.630
In the source block, metamorphic basement precedes the granite intrusion, and

00:01:06.630 --> 00:01:10.290
erosion truncates both before younger sediment accumulates.

00:01:10.630 --> 00:01:14.830
Later dikes, lava, faults and canyon erosion add events.

00:01:15.070 --> 00:01:17.550
Use actual contacts to build a partial order.

00:01:17.910 --> 00:01:21.650
Numbered source events are a teaching reconstruction, not a rule that

00:01:21.650 --> 00:01:23.950
all faults everywhere form in the same stage.

00:01:24.890 --> 00:01:27.910
The source lava is offset by a fault scarp, so that

00:01:27.910 --> 00:01:29.330
movement is younger than the lava.

00:01:29.570 --> 00:01:32.770
Earlier movement may have helped create relief before the eruption.

00:01:33.130 --> 00:01:36.170
River gravel that is not offset provides a later constraint.

00:01:36.410 --> 00:01:38.350
A feature can have more than one episode;

00:01:38.730 --> 00:01:41.830
write the dated movement rather than giving the entire fault one

00:01:41.830 --> 00:01:43.090
oversimplified age.
